// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["ncfm-cmd"] = {"qs": [["Clearing & Settlement", "Member X, on behalf of clients, has bought 1,000 shares of a stock and sold 700 shares of the same stock in the same settlement (all trades for one client). X's net securities obligation is to:", ["Receive 300 shares", "Receive 1,000 shares and deliver 700 shares separately", "Deliver 1,700 shares", "Deliver 300 shares"], 0, "Net position = 1,000 bought − 700 sold = 300 shares to be received. Netting replaces separate gross movements of 1,000 and 700 shares with a single receipt."], ["Trading System & Orders", "An order with the 'Day' validity condition that is not executed by the close of trading will:", ["Be cancelled automatically at the end of the day", "Be executed compulsorily at the official closing price of the day", "Be carried forward to the next trading day", "Remain pending until the trader cancels it"], 0, "A Day order is valid only for the trading day on which it is entered. Unexecuted portions are cancelled by the system at the end of the session, not carried forward or forced to execute."], ["Fundamental Valuation Concepts", "Company A has a debt-equity ratio of 0.5 and Company B has 2.0, with similar businesses. Which statement is correct?", ["Both have the same financial risk as the businesses are similar", "Company B has no debt at all", "Company A is more leveraged than Company B", "Company B is more leveraged and carries higher financial risk"], 3, "Debt-equity measures borrowed funds relative to owners' funds. B has ₹2 of debt per ₹1 of equity, versus ₹0.50 for A, so B has higher leverage and financial risk."], ["Clearing & Settlement", "Which entity moves funds between clearing members' accounts and the clearing corporation's account during settlement?", ["Clearing banks", "Registrars and transfer agents", "Merchant bankers", "Depository participants"], 0, "Funds settlement takes place through designated clearing banks, where members maintain clearing accounts. Depositories and their participants handle securities, while RTAs and merchant bankers have no role in funds pay-in."], ["Legal Framework", "Which law contains provisions on the listing of securities and the conditions under which contracts in securities are legal?", ["Indian Contract Act, 1872 alone", "Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956", "Depositories Act, 1996", "Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002 and its rules"], 1, "The SCRA governs recognised stock exchanges, contracts in securities and listing. The Depositories Act covers electronic holding, and PMLA deals with money laundering."], ["Legal Framework", "A dispute between a client and a trading member about a trade that is not resolved through the exchange's grievance mechanism can be referred to:", ["The Insurance Ombudsman", "Arbitration under the exchange's bye-laws", "The Registrar of Companies", "The Banking Ombudsman of RBI"], 1, "Exchange bye-laws provide arbitration for disputes between members and clients. Insurance and banking ombudsmen handle other sectors, and the Registrar of Companies deals with company law filings."], ["Trading Membership", "The Risk Disclosure Document given to a client during registration mainly explains:", ["The tax rates applicable to the client's capital gains", "The risks involved in trading in securities and derivatives", "The list of shares recommended by the broker for purchase", "The broker's own financial statements for the last three years"], 1, "The Risk Disclosure Document informs the client of the risks of trading, such as price volatility and leverage. It is not a statement of the broker's finances, tax advice or a stock recommendation."], ["Trading Membership", "Which of the following is a typical eligibility requirement for an individual or corporate seeking trading membership?", ["Being registered as a banking company with RBI", "Holding a minimum of 1% of the exchange's shares", "Having at least ten years of experience as a listed company director", "Meeting the exchange's net worth and deposit norms and being a fit and proper person"], 3, "Exchanges prescribe financial criteria such as net worth and deposits, along with fit-and-proper and qualification requirements. Holding exchange shares, banking registration or directorship experience are not general conditions for membership."], ["Trading System & Orders", "A trader places a limit buy order for 500 shares at ₹120. The best sell orders are 200 shares at ₹119 and 400 shares at ₹121. What happens to the order on entry?", ["500 shares are bought at an average price of ₹120", "200 shares are bought at ₹119 and 300 shares remain pending at ₹120", "200 shares are bought at ₹119 and 300 shares are bought at ₹121", "No shares are bought because the full quantity is not available at ₹120"], 1, "A limit buy at ₹120 can match only sell orders at ₹120 or below, so 200 shares trade at ₹119. The remaining 300 shares stay in the book at ₹120 because ₹121 is above the limit."], ["Clearing & Settlement", "Under a T+1 settlement cycle, if a trade is executed on a Thursday and Friday is a normal working day, the settlement takes place on:", ["Tuesday", "Monday", "Thursday itself", "Friday"], 3, "T+1 means settlement occurs one working day after the trade date. A Thursday trade is therefore settled on Friday, assuming Friday is a settlement working day."], ["Fundamental Valuation Concepts", "A company has just paid a dividend of ₹4 per share. Dividends are expected to grow at 6% a year forever, and investors require a 12% return. Using the constant-growth model, the share's value is about:", ["₹35.33", "₹33.33", "₹66.67", "₹70.67"], 3, "Next year's dividend D1 = ₹4 × 1.06 = ₹4.24. Value = D1 ÷ (r − g) = ₹4.24 ÷ 0.06 = ₹70.67. ₹66.67 wrongly uses the current dividend instead of D1."], ["Fundamental Valuation Concepts", "A share has EPS of ₹12 and pays a dividend of ₹3 per share. Its dividend payout ratio is:", ["25%", "30%", "75%", "4%"], 0, "Payout ratio = DPS ÷ EPS = ₹3 ÷ ₹12 = 25%. The remaining 75% is the retention ratio, which is the tempting wrong option."], ["Trading Membership", "A client wants to trade through a broker's authorised person. Who is responsible to the client for the authorised person's acts in relation to the business?", ["Only the authorised person, in his personal capacity, and never the trading member", "The trading member who appointed the authorised person", "The depository where the client's shares are held", "The stock exchange that approved the appointment"], 1, "The trading member is responsible for all acts of its authorised persons in connection with the securities business. The exchange approves the appointment but does not take over the member's responsibility."], ["Securities Market Basics", "Preference shareholders differ from equity shareholders mainly because preference shareholders:", ["Receive dividend only after all equity shareholders have been paid", "Get priority over equity holders for dividend and capital repayment on winding up", "Always enjoy full voting rights on every resolution placed before the company's general meetings", "Have no claim at all on the company's assets on liquidation"], 1, "Preference shares carry preferential rights to dividend and to repayment of capital ahead of equity shares. Their voting rights are generally limited to matters directly affecting them, unlike equity shareholders."], ["Legal Framework", "Under anti-money-laundering requirements, an intermediary must identify the 'beneficial owner' of a client account in order to:", ["Know the natural person who ultimately owns or controls the client", "Calculate the brokerage payable on each trade", "Determine the tax rate applicable to the account", "Decide whether the client is eligible for allotment in the retail category of IPOs and FPOs"], 0, "PMLA rules require intermediaries to identify the natural persons who ultimately own or control a client, especially companies and trusts. This prevents misuse of layered entities to launder money."], ["Trading Membership", "Which statement about a client's power of attorney or demat debit authorisation given to a broker is correct?", ["It allows the broker to transfer the client's shares for any purpose", "It permits the broker to pledge client shares with lenders to raise the broker's own working capital", "It is compulsory for every client before trading can start", "It is limited to purposes such as meeting the client's own settlement and margin obligations"], 3, "SEBI has restricted such authorisations to specific uses linked to the client's own obligations, and they cannot be made mandatory. Using them for the broker's own funding or any other purpose is prohibited."], ["Legal Framework", "Two brokers repeatedly trade a thinly traded stock between themselves at rising prices to create a false appearance of activity. This practice is called:", ["Arbitrage, a legitimate trading strategy", "Hedging, permitted for risk management", "Market making, encouraged by exchanges", "Circular trading, a manipulative practice under PFUTP Regulations"], 3, "Trading among a group to create artificial volumes and prices is circular trading, prohibited under SEBI's PFUTP Regulations. Arbitrage and hedging are legitimate strategies that do not create false market appearance."], ["Trading System & Orders", "Which of the following orders is placed to sell at the best available price immediately, without specifying a price?", ["Market order", "Disclosed quantity order", "Stop-loss limit order", "Limit order"], 0, "A market order carries no price and executes against the best available counter orders. A limit order specifies the worst acceptable price, and a stop-loss order waits for a trigger."], ["Securities Market Basics", "Which of these investors is MOST exposed to the company's business risk?", ["A secured debenture holder of the company", "A bank that has lent to the company against property", "A fixed deposit holder of the company", "An equity shareholder of the company"], 3, "Equity holders are residual claimants: they are paid only after lenders and creditors, so their returns depend directly on business performance. Secured lenders and debenture holders have contractual claims and security, which reduces their risk."], ["Risk Management & Margins", "Why is the capital adequacy requirement imposed on trading members?", ["To fix the maximum brokerage a member can charge", "To ensure that members have enough resources to absorb losses and meet obligations", "To pay dividends to the exchange's shareholders", "To decide the number of clients a member may register"], 1, "Capital adequacy norms require members to keep base and additional capital with the clearing corporation, which acts as a cushion against losses and default. They are not related to brokerage limits or client numbers."], ["Securities Market Basics", "A rights issue is offered at a price below the current market price. An existing shareholder who does not wish to subscribe can usually:", ["Renounce the rights entitlement in favour of another person", "Demand that the company buy back the existing shares", "Ask the exchange to cancel the rights issue for all holders", "Convert the entitlement into a bonus share at no cost"], 0, "Rights entitlements are generally renounceable, so a shareholder can sell or transfer them to someone else. This lets the holder capture some value instead of simply letting the entitlement lapse."], ["Market Participants", "Which intermediary manages a public issue, prepares the offer document and coordinates with SEBI and the exchanges?", ["A merchant banker acting as book running lead manager", "A trading member of the stock exchange on which the shares will list", "A depository participant", "A registrar to the issue"], 0, "The book running lead manager, a SEBI-registered merchant banker, manages the issue process, does due diligence and files the offer document. The registrar handles applications and allotment, which is a separate function."], ["Legal Framework", "SEBI's powers under the SEBI Act, 1992 include:", ["Fixing the interest rate on bank deposits", "Collecting income tax on capital gains", "Issuing currency notes in India", "Registering and regulating intermediaries and imposing penalties for violations"], 3, "SEBI registers and regulates market intermediaries, conducts inspections and investigations, and imposes penalties. Interest rates and currency are RBI matters, while tax collection rests with the Income Tax Department."], ["Securities Market Basics", "Which of the following is a secondary market transaction?", ["An investor buys shares of a listed bank from another investor through the exchange", "A company allots shares to applicants in its initial public offer", "A listed company places fresh shares with institutions through a qualified institutional placement", "A listed company issues additional shares to existing holders in a rights issue"], 0, "In the secondary market, already-issued securities change hands between investors, and the company receives no money. IPOs, rights issues and QIPs are all primary market issues that raise fresh capital for the company."], ["Trading System & Orders", "A trader holding shares bought at ₹500 wants to limit losses if the price falls to ₹470. Which order suits this need?", ["A stop-loss buy order with a trigger price of ₹530", "A limit buy order at ₹470", "A stop-loss sell order with trigger price ₹470", "A market buy order placed immediately"], 2, "A stop-loss sell order becomes active only when the price falls to the trigger, protecting the holder against further loss. A buy order would add to the position instead of limiting the loss."], ["Risk Management & Margins", "A market-wide circuit breaker is triggered by:", ["A member failing to pay margins", "A single stock hitting its price band", "The clearing corporation's settlement guarantee fund falling below its minimum required corpus", "A sharp movement in a broad market index such as Nifty 50 or Sensex"], 3, "Market-wide circuit breakers apply to the whole equity market and are triggered by large moves in the main indices, halting trading for set periods. Individual stock price bands work separately."], ["Fundamental Valuation Concepts", "A company's net profit is ₹84 crore and it has 12 crore shares. Its share trades at ₹105. What is the P/E ratio?", ["8.75", "15", "7", "12"], 1, "EPS = ₹84 crore ÷ 12 crore = ₹7. P/E = ₹105 ÷ ₹7 = 15. The option 7 is the EPS itself, and 8.75 wrongly divides price by the number of shares."], ["Market Participants", "Who is responsible for the guaranteed settlement of trades executed on a stock exchange?", ["The issuing company", "The registrar and transfer agent", "The clearing corporation", "The depository"], 2, "The clearing corporation clears and settles trades and guarantees settlement through novation and its settlement guarantee fund. Depositories only hold and transfer securities in electronic form on its instructions."], ["Clearing & Settlement", "'Pay-out' in the settlement process refers to:", ["Members depositing funds and securities with the clearing corporation", "The clearing corporation releasing funds and securities to members entitled to receive them", "A client paying brokerage to the trading member", "The company paying dividends to shareholders"], 1, "Pay-in is when members deliver funds and securities; pay-out is the reverse, when the clearing corporation releases them to receiving members, who then pass them on to clients."], ["Fundamental Valuation Concepts", "₹50,000 is invested at 10% per annum compounded annually. Its value after 3 years is:", ["₹66,000", "₹65,000", "₹66,550", "₹60,500"], 2, "Future value = ₹50,000 × (1.10)³ = ₹50,000 × 1.331 = ₹66,550. ₹65,000 is the simple interest answer, which ignores interest on interest."], ["Clearing & Settlement", "Novation helps market participants mainly by:", ["Fixing the price at which all trades settle", "Eliminating the need for margins", "Removing counterparty risk between individual trading members", "Allowing members to cancel their trades unilaterally even after the settlement is complete"], 2, "Through novation the clearing corporation becomes buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer, so members are not exposed to each other's default. Margins are still needed because the clearing corporation bears that risk."], ["Trading System & Orders", "In the buy side of an order book there are bids at ₹305 (time 10:01), ₹305 (time 10:00) and ₹306 (time 10:02). An incoming market sell order for a small quantity matches first with the bid at:", ["₹305 entered at 10:01", "The earliest bid, regardless of price", "₹306 entered at 10:02", "₹305 entered at 10:00"], 2, "Price priority comes first, so the highest bid of ₹306 gets matched before the ₹305 bids, even though it was entered later. Time priority applies only between orders at the same price."], ["Market Participants", "A company wants an agency to maintain its register of shareholders, process transfers and send dividend warrants. It should appoint a:", ["Merchant Banker", "Registrar and Share Transfer Agent", "Depository Participant of the investors", "Clearing Member"], 1, "Registrars and transfer agents (RTAs) maintain the records of a company's shareholders and handle investor servicing such as dividends and transfers. A depository participant serves investors' demat accounts, not the issuer's register."], ["Clearing & Settlement", "A client buys 600 shares at ₹80 and sells 600 shares of the same stock at ₹82 on the same day in the same account. At settlement:", ["The client pays ₹1,200 as the trade is reversed", "No shares change hands and the client receives the profit of ₹1,200", "The client must deliver 600 shares to the clearing corporation and receive ₹49,200 in return", "The client receives 600 shares and pays ₹48,000"], 1, "Buying and selling the same quantity on the same day nets to zero securities. Only the difference of 600 × (₹82 − ₹80) = ₹1,200 is settled, received by the client as profit."], ["Market Participants", "Which of these is primarily an institutional investor rather than an intermediary?", ["A custodian holding securities for clients", "A depository participant opening and servicing demat accounts for retail clients", "A stock broker executing orders for clients", "An insurance company investing its policyholders' funds"], 3, "An insurer invests its own and policyholders' money, so it is an investor. Brokers, depository participants and custodians provide services to other investors and are intermediaries in the market."], ["Risk Management & Margins", "A client short-sold 300 shares at ₹210 in the cash market during the day. The closing price is ₹216. The mark-to-market loss on this position is:", ["₹2,100", "₹6,300", "₹1,200", "₹1,800"], 3, "For a short (sell) position, a rise in price is a loss. MTM loss = 300 × (₹216 − ₹210) = ₹1,800. ₹6,300 wrongly multiplies 300 by 21 instead of the price difference."], ["Market Participants", "A person appointed by a trading member to provide access to the trading platform as its agent, without being a member himself, is called a/an:", ["Sub-depository", "Authorised person", "Custodial participant", "Professional clearing member"], 1, "An authorised person is appointed by a trading member, with exchange approval, to offer access to the trading platform as the member's agent. The trading member remains responsible for the authorised person's acts."], ["Risk Management & Margins", "For a purchase worth ₹8,00,000, the VaR margin rate is 10% and the extreme loss margin rate is 3.5%. The total of these two margins is:", ["₹1,08,000", "₹28,000", "₹1,35,000", "₹80,000"], 0, "Total margin = ₹8,00,000 × (10% + 3.5%) = ₹8,00,000 × 13.5% = ₹1,08,000. ₹80,000 is only the VaR margin and ₹28,000 is only the extreme loss margin."], ["Trading Membership", "A trading member receives a complaint from a client about an unauthorised trade. The most appropriate first step is to:", ["Ignore it unless the amount involved exceeds ₹1 lakh", "Ask the client to approach the stock exchange directly", "Record and investigate the complaint and resolve it within the prescribed timeline", "Close the client's account to avoid further disputes"], 2, "Members must maintain a grievance redressal mechanism, register complaints and resolve them promptly. A client may escalate to the exchange or SEBI's SCORES only if not satisfied, so referring him away first is incorrect."], ["Trading Membership", "Before executing a client's first order, a trading member must ensure that:", ["The client has deposited at least one year's brokerage in advance", "The client has completed a training course conducted by the exchange", "KYC is completed and the client registration documents are executed", "The client has opened a bank account with the same broker"], 2, "Client registration, including KYC and the rights and obligations documents, must be completed before trading. There is no requirement of advance brokerage, exchange training or a bank account with the broker."], ["Legal Framework", "The Depositories Act, 1996 primarily enabled:", ["Mandatory listing of all public companies", "The creation of clearing corporations", "Holding and transfer of securities in electronic book-entry form", "The setting up of SEBI as a statutory body"], 2, "The Depositories Act allowed securities to be held in dematerialised form and transferred by book entry through depositories. SEBI's statutory status came from the SEBI Act, 1992."], ["Fundamental Valuation Concepts", "A company's net worth is ₹450 crore and it has 15 crore shares outstanding. Its book value per share is:", ["₹30", "₹15", "₹45", "₹67.50"], 0, "Book value per share = net worth ÷ number of shares = ₹450 crore ÷ 15 crore = ₹30. The other options come from mixing up the two figures."], ["Legal Framework", "Under the SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations, 'unpublished price sensitive information' includes:", ["The company's name and registered office address", "The closing price of the company's share on the exchange", "Information on a proposed merger not yet disclosed to the stock exchanges", "Quarterly results already filed with the exchanges"], 2, "UPSI is information that is not generally available and is likely to materially affect the price. Undisclosed merger plans qualify; filed results and public data are generally available information."], ["Trading Membership", "Why must each order entered on the trading system carry the client's Unique Client Code?", ["So that every trade can be traced to the actual client for surveillance and settlement", "So that the exchange can charge a separate listing fee per client", "So that the client's PAN is shown publicly on the order book", "So that orders from clients get priority over proprietary orders"], 0, "The UCC links each order to a registered client, enabling audit trail, surveillance and correct allocation. Client identity is not displayed publicly, and UCC does not affect order priority or fees."], ["Trading Membership", "A trading member uses securities lying in a client's account to meet the margin obligations of another client. This is:", ["Prohibited, because client securities must be used only for that client", "Allowed if the broker returns the securities within a week", "Allowed if both clients belong to the same family", "Allowed for retail clients but not for institutions"], 0, "Client assets must be segregated and used only for the respective client's obligations. Using one client's securities for another's margin is misuse of client assets and is prohibited regardless of when they are returned."], ["Clearing & Settlement", "Netting of obligations by the clearing corporation means that:", ["Trades are cancelled if the member cannot pay", "Profits of one member are used to pay losses of another member", "Each member's buys and sells in a security are offset to arrive at one net amount to pay or receive", "Every trade is settled separately and gross with each counterparty"], 2, "Multilateral netting combines all trades of a member in a security into a single net position for funds and securities. This reduces settlement volumes, unlike gross trade-by-trade settlement."], ["Risk Management & Margins", "Value at Risk (VaR) margin is computed using:", ["Statistical estimates of the likely maximum loss over a short horizon at a high confidence level", "The face value of the share multiplied by a fixed 50%", "The dividend history of the company over five years", "The book value per share of the company"], 0, "VaR margin is based on the volatility of the security and estimates potential loss at a chosen confidence level (such as 99%) over a short period. Face value, book value and dividends are not inputs."], ["Fundamental Valuation Concepts", "A share priced at ₹160 has EPS of ₹8. Its earnings yield is:", ["2%", "20%", "5%", "8%"], 2, "Earnings yield = EPS ÷ price = ₹8 ÷ ₹160 = 5%, the inverse of the P/E ratio of 20. The option 20% confuses P/E with earnings yield."], ["Fundamental Valuation Concepts", "What is the present value of ₹10,000 received at the end of each year for 3 years, if the discount rate is 10% per annum?", ["About ₹27,273", "About ₹24,869", "₹30,000", "About ₹22,539"], 1, "PV = 10,000/1.1 + 10,000/1.1² + 10,000/1.1³ = 9,091 + 8,264 + 7,513 ≈ ₹24,869. ₹30,000 ignores discounting altogether."], ["Risk Management & Margins", "A broker allows a client to trade without collecting the required upfront margin, planning to collect it the next day. Under current SEBI norms this is:", ["Permitted if the client signs an undertaking", "Permitted, but only in the cash segment and only for delivery-based trades of retail clients", "Not permitted, and short collection of margin attracts penalty", "Permitted for clients with a good track record"], 2, "SEBI requires margins to be collected from clients upfront, and exchanges levy penalties on short or non-collection. Track record or undertakings do not exempt the broker from this requirement."], ["Securities Market Basics", "An investor holds 200 shares of face value ₹10 bought at ₹900. The company splits each share into five shares of face value ₹2. Ignoring market movement, after the split the investor holds:", ["200 shares worth about ₹180 each", "1,000 shares worth about ₹180 each", "1,000 shares worth about ₹900 each", "40 shares worth about ₹4,500 each"], 1, "A 1:5 split multiplies the number of shares by five (200 × 5 = 1,000) and divides the price by five (₹900 ÷ 5 = ₹180). Total investment value remains unchanged at ₹1,80,000."], ["Market Participants", "Foreign portfolio investors in India are registered with SEBI through:", ["Designated depository participants authorised by SEBI", "The Reserve Bank of India's regional offices and the authorised dealer banks", "Any trading member of a recognised exchange", "The Ministry of Corporate Affairs"], 0, "SEBI has authorised designated depository participants (DDPs) to grant FPI registration on its behalf. Brokers execute trades but do not register FPIs, and RBI does not handle FPI registration."], ["Legal Framework", "An employee of a listed company buys shares of the company after hearing about an unannounced big order from a colleague. He argues he was not involved in the deal. Under insider trading rules he:", ["Can be treated as an insider because he possessed unpublished price sensitive information", "Is safe because he heard it informally from a colleague and did not see any official company document", "Is safe as long as he holds the shares for over a year", "Is safe because only directors can be insiders"], 0, "Anyone who has access to or possesses UPSI can be an insider, regardless of designation or how the information was received. Trading while in possession of UPSI is prohibited, and holding period is irrelevant."], ["Market Participants", "An investor's shares are held electronically in a depository. Which entity actually provides the investor with account statements and executes instructions such as delivery instructions?", ["The registrar of the issuing company", "The stock exchange on which the shares are listed", "The depository participant with whom the account is opened", "The clearing corporation of the exchange"], 2, "Investors interact with the depository through a depository participant, which opens the demat account, accepts delivery instructions and sends statements. The depository itself does not deal directly with individual investors."], ["Securities Market Basics", "A company with 100 crore shares trading at ₹250 has promoters holding 60%. Assuming the entire non-promoter holding is free float, its free-float market capitalisation is:", ["₹25,000 crore", "₹6,000 crore", "₹15,000 crore", "₹10,000 crore"], 3, "Free-float shares = 40% of 100 crore = 40 crore. Multiplying by ₹250 gives ₹10,000 crore. ₹25,000 crore is full market capitalisation and ₹15,000 crore wrongly uses the promoter stake."], ["Risk Management & Margins", "Mark-to-market margin is collected to cover:", ["The cost of opening a demat account", "Potential losses in a future market crash only", "The notional loss on open positions based on the day's closing price", "The brokerage payable by the client"], 2, "MTM margin covers the actual loss already incurred on open positions compared with the closing price. Future extreme moves are covered by VaR and extreme loss margins, not by MTM."], ["Trading System & Orders", "A seller enters 10,000 shares with a disclosed quantity of 1,000. What does the market see?", ["The order is hidden until the full 10,000 shares can be matched", "All 10,000 shares are displayed, but only 1,000 can trade per day", "Only 1,000 shares are sold and the rest are cancelled", "Only 1,000 shares are displayed at a time and the next 1,000 show after each lot trades"], 3, "Disclosed quantity reveals only part of a large order to the market. Once the disclosed portion is executed, the next portion is shown, until the full order is filled or cancelled."], ["Trading System & Orders", "Why do exchanges apply price bands on individual stocks?", ["To guarantee that every order gets executed within the day", "To curb excessive volatility by rejecting orders outside a permitted range", "To prevent investors from placing market orders", "To fix the price at which the stock must close every day"], 1, "Price bands restrict orders to a range around a reference price so that sharp, possibly erroneous or manipulative moves are checked. They neither guarantee execution nor fix the closing price."], ["Securities Market Basics", "A bonus issue in the ratio 1:1 is announced. Which statement correctly describes its effect?", ["The company's total net worth doubles because new capital is raised", "The face value of each existing share is halved to create new shares", "The number of shares doubles, while reserves are capitalised and no cash is paid by shareholders", "Shareholders must pay the face value in cash for each additional share received under the bonus issue"], 2, "A bonus issue converts free reserves into share capital. Shareholders get one free share for each held, so the share count doubles, but net worth is unchanged. Halving face value describes a split, not a bonus."], ["Securities Market Basics", "An exchange traded fund (ETF) tracking the Nifty 50 is best described as a:", ["Derivative contract that expires on the last Thursday of each month", "Debt instrument issued by the exchange to track index returns", "Fund whose units trade on the exchange throughout the day at market prices", "Fund whose units can be bought only directly from the AMC at the NAV declared at the end of the day"], 2, "ETF units are listed and traded on stock exchanges like shares, at prices that change during the day. Buying only from the AMC at end-of-day NAV describes ordinary open-ended mutual fund schemes."], ["Risk Management & Margins", "A stock with previous close of ₹640 is in a 10% price band. Which of these sell orders will be rejected by the system?", ["Sell at ₹700", "Sell at ₹712", "Sell at ₹580", "Sell at ₹640"], 1, "A 10% band on ₹640 allows prices from ₹576 to ₹704. ₹712 is above the upper limit, so the order is rejected. ₹580, ₹640 and ₹700 all fall inside the range."], ["Trading System & Orders", "In NSE's trading system, when a passive order already in the book is matched with an incoming active order, the trade takes place at the:", ["Last traded price of the stock at the time of the previous trade", "Average of the two order prices", "Price of the incoming active order", "Price of the passive order already in the book"], 3, "Trades execute at the price of the order resting in the book (passive order). The incoming order may thus get a better price than its limit, which is why using the active order's price is wrong."], ["Clearing & Settlement", "If a buyer does not receive shares because the selling member defaulted on delivery and the auction also fails, the position is usually:", ["Cancelled with no compensation to the buyer", "Carried forward indefinitely to the next settlement until the shares become available in the market", "Settled by the depository from its own holdings", "Closed out at a price determined under the clearing corporation's rules"], 3, "When an auction cannot obtain the shares, the shortage is closed out and the buyer is compensated in cash at a close-out price set under clearing corporation norms. The depository does not supply shares itself."], ["Market Participants", "A clearing member who clears and settles only his own trades and those of his clients is called a:", ["Authorised person", "Professional clearing member", "Custodial participant", "Self-clearing member"], 3, "A self-clearing member clears only its own (proprietary and client) trades. A professional clearing member clears trades of other trading members and custodial participants, which is the tempting but wrong choice."]]};
